  "tour": {
    "title": "From Heaven to Resurrection: What Happens to the Body?",
    "intro": "This tour follows the New Testament’s sequence rather than collapsing everything into the phrase “going to heaven.” It starts by clarifying what resurrection means, then considers the interval after death, the character of the resurrected body, and the New Testament’s larger hope for creation. The teachers are broadly aligned, though they acknowledge mystery and some interpretive questions along the way.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:6a253c6-com-651c11dc-e7c9-3e9c-8d28-dfdec7d811d5",
        "startSec": 670,
        "endSec": 790,
        "why": "Begin here because it names the central distinction in your question. N.T. Wright says, “Resurrection is not about somebody going into a glorious angelic state” and that the word does not mean “going to heaven”; he then connects Jesus’ empty tomb and recognizable wounds to the hope for our bodies.",
        "episodeTitle": "#164 Easter questions on the resurrection of Jesus",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR6878813537.mp3?updated=1749655811",
        "durationSec": 2012,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:7dbd0c70-com-ad14c96d-7e81-3bdd-a910-a23bb2f5b5f9",
        "startSec": 220,
        "endSec": 400,
        "why": "This clip handles the in-between question without making it the final destination. The commentator notes that 2 Corinthians 5 has “a lack of precise timeline,” but reads it alongside Paul’s other letters as “life, death, being with the Lord, and resurrection,” explaining the present body as an “earthly tent” that is temporary.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Corinthians 5:1-10",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/jckmzx/2_corinthians_51-10816em.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1584,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:ade907de-com-c0dd0b4b-3d8e-3388-83cf-7b219a3bf918",
        "startSec": 450,
        "endSec": 630,
        "why": "Listen next for a careful answer to what death is like before resurrection. Wright argues that the interval with Christ will be conscious—“surrounded by and bathed in love”—while still saying that bodily death followed by bodily life is such a sharp contrast that “sleep and wake” remains a useful image.",
        "episodeTitle": "#106 Is cremation or burial best?",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR1327536790.mp3?updated=1749817404",
        "durationSec": 1488,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:8380ffa3-com-b72975ac-389f-3ffa-84fb-478fb64f2873",
        "startSec": 900,
        "endSec": 1190,
        "why": "Now turn to Paul’s description of the resurrected body in 1 Corinthians 15. The commentator explains that the body is sown perishable but raised imperishable, and that “spiritual body” does not mean immaterial or disembodied: it is still a body, empowered and fitted for “supernatural life in the world to come.”",
        "episodeTitle": "1 Corinthians 15:29-58",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/d8ngn3/1_corinthians_1529-589yhwk.mp3",
        "durationSec": 2010,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:heaven-earth-q-r",
        "startSec": 1560,
        "endSec": 1740,
        "why": "End by widening the horizon beyond an individual soul’s destination. BibleProject traces the New Testament hope toward “the complete reunion of heaven and earth,” culminating in Jesus’ return, so resurrection belongs to the restoration of creation rather than escape from it.",
        "episodeTitle": "Heaven & Earth Question & Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e377fa21-f15c-4ec8-b498-507f52e60c14/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e377fa21-f15c-4ec8-b498-507f52e60c14",
        "durationSec": 2671,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips present a clear New Testament pattern: death is not treated as the completion of salvation; believers may be with Christ in an intermediate state, but the ultimate hope is resurrection—bodily, transformed, imperishable life when God renews creation. The details remain mysterious, but the direction is not: Jesus’ empty tomb and risen body are the pattern and promise.",
    "totalSeconds": 950
  },
